sampling gaps; the gaps between separate land masses or isolated parts of
the range are much longer. L is the last link before the largest relative
jump in the sorted spanning-tree links between \code{link_search[1]} and
\code{link_search[2]} km. A second distance L2 is used for corridors: the link
just after the largest jump among the links longer than L (up to
\code{link2_max_km}), or the longest of them when there are only one or two.
See \code{\link[=linking_distance]{linking_distance()}}.

\strong{Methods} (radius r = L/2 by default: two buffers of L/2 touch when
records are L apart, so every chain of records linked at L becomes one
patch):
\itemize{
\item \code{"delaunay"} (default): Delaunay triangles whose edges are all at most L,
plus buffers of r around every record. Follows concave range outlines.
\item \code{"buffer"}: buffers of radius \code{buffer_km} (default r) around every
record.
\item \code{"cluster_hull"}: for each cluster of records linked at L, the convex
hull (\code{hull_ratio = 1}) or a concave hull (\code{hull_ratio < 1}) of the
buffers. Fills gaps inside each cluster automatically.
\item \code{"convex_hull"}: one convex hull around all buffers (the simplest and
most generous M).
}

\strong{Steps after the method}, in order: corridors between clusters
